    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/faculty-position-in-teacher-education-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Teacher Education",
      "description": "Faculty Position in Teacher Education - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Faculty Position in Teacher Education Stanford University University Tenure Line Opening at: Sep 1 2026 - 3:05pm PDT Closing at: Oct 1 2026 - 11:55pm PDT Add to favorites View favorites The Stanford Graduate School of Education (GSE) seeks to fill a faculty position in Teacher Education at the associate or full professor rank. We welcome applications from established scholars with distinction and an active and rigorous research agenda in the field of teacher education, broadly conceived. Candidates’ research and practice should address issues of teaching and teacher education. Engagement in teacher education policy is desirable. The successful candidate will be expected to play a leadership role in Stanford’s Teacher Education Program (STEP) in close collaboration with STEP’s senior leadership team, among other contributions to the GSE including teaching and advising at the masters and doctoral levels. The GSE faculty represents considerable diversity, including in its theoretical, methodological, and substantive expertise and interests. We seek a colleague who can work effectively within this multidisciplinary community. All applicants should provide the following: a brief cover letter a combined research and teaching statement (no more than 3 pages total) curriculum vitae three scholarly publications and/or well-developed papers the names of three references, who may be contacted for a letter at a later stage in the application process All application materials must be submitted online. Please submit your application to the following site: https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/faculty-position-in-teacher-education-stanford-university-california-united-states Application deadline is October 1, 2026 at 11:55pm PST. Questions pertaining to this position may be directed to Tanya Chamberlain, Faculty Affairs, tanyas@stanford.edu . Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . The expected base pay range for this position is: Assoc. Prof: $160k – $180k Full Prof: $200k - $275k Back to all jobs Apply Now First Name (required) 423c2e71 Last Name (required) 6b23eaa1 Email (required) e6ee15be Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 680ae65f Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. School Graduate School of Education Graduate School of Education Line University Tenure Line University Tenure Line Location Stanford University, California, United States Stanford University, California, United States First Name (required) 34c7efaa Last Name (required) ab9054eb Email (required) 2a380917 Subscribe Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
    },
    "https://facultypositions.stanford.edu/jobs/thoracic-surgery-division-chief-stanford-university-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Open Faculty Positions",
      "description": "Thoracic Surgery Division Chief - Stanford University, California, United States Stanford Faculty Positions Site navigation Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode Open Faculty Positions Thoracic Surgery Division Chief Stanford University University Medical Line University Tenure Line Opening at: Aug 31 2026 - 9:00am PDT Add to favorites View favorites The Division of Thoracic Surgery in the Department of Cardiothoracic Surgery at Stanford University seeks a board certified Thoracic Surgeon to join the Cardiothoracic Surgery Department as Thoracic Surgery Division Chief and Associate Professor or Professor in the University Medical Line or University Tenure Line. We are particularly interested in candidates who have demonstrated experience in academic leadership, excellence in thoracic surgical care, a strong record of scholarly research, and a commitment to faculty development, mentorship, and the education of residents and fellows. • The major criteria for appointment for faculty in the University Medical Line shall be excellence in the overall mix of clinical care, clinical teaching, scholarly activity that advances clinical medicine, and institutional service appropriate to the programmatic need the individual is expected to fulfill. • The predominant criterion for appointment in the University Tenure Line is a major commitment to research and teaching. Faculty rank and line will be determined by the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate. The successful applicant should have an MD or equivalent degree, board certification in Thoracic Surgery, eligibility for medical licensure in the State of California, and a record of excellence and prominence in patient care, research, and education. Demonstrated experience in academic leadership, teaching, mentorship, and faculty development is also expected. The Thoracic Surgery Division Chief within the Cardiothoracic Surgery Department will lead a large team of high-performing faculty and staff dedicated to clinical excellence, innovative research, and worldclass education. The Department is seeking a surgeon-scientist leader who possesses prominence in patient care, published scholarship in basic, translational, engineering, clinical and/or population science, and a commitment to teaching the next generation. The Division Chief will report to the Chair of the Department of Cardiothoracic Surgery. The Division Chief role comprises administrative duties and will participate in Department activities and appropriate activities of the School of Medicine and the Stanford Healthcare System. Because programmatic needs and circumstances can evolve, the Division Chief's duties and responsibilities as described here may change; should that occur, we will discuss priorities and determine how best to achieve our shared goals. The Division Chief will work to build on the division’s strengths to foster an outstanding Thoracic Division that excels in the three missions of clinical care, research, and education. The Division Chief will anticipate developing and supporting Thoracic Surgery programs that position Stanford as a preeminent leader in the field. The Division Chief will implement and support robust faculty development, mentorship, and succession planning, with a strong emphasis on engagement. Recruitment, retention, mentoring, and development of faculty—especially junior faculty—as well as trainees, is a central component of the role. The Division Chief will champion quality improvement and patient-safety initiatives, collaborate with other divisions and centers to advance programmatic goals in thoracic oncology, minimally invasive thoracic surgery, robotics, and related areas as appropriate. Division Chief will strategically support, invest in, and cultivate novel research within the Thoracic Division to strengthen the division’s research portfolio and align with the department and institution’s research priorities. The Division Chief will foster educational opportunities for residents and fellows/instructors. The activities of the Division and Department are diverse and encompass complex clinical care, innovative research, and the education and training of future leaders in cardiothoracic surgery, offering unique opportunities for the successful candidate to expand multidisciplinary programs, strengthen the Division's research portfolio, develop faculty and trainees, and shape the future of thoracic surgery at Stanford. Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . The university’s central functions of research and education depend on freedom of thought, and expression. The Department of Cardiothoracic Surgery, School of Medicine, and Stanford University value faculty who will help foster an open and respectful academic environment for colleagues, students, and staff with a wide range of backgrounds, identities, and perspectives. Candidates may choose to include as part of their research and teaching statements a brief discussion about how their work and experience will further these values. Submit CV, a brief letter and the names of three references to: https://secure.dc4.pageuppeople.com/apply/1054/gateway/Default.aspx?c=apply&sJobIDs=495032&SourceTypeID=796&sLanguage=en-us For questions, please contact Eunice Lin, Faculty Affairs Administrator, at Eunice.lin@stanford.edu . The expected base pay range for this position is: Associate Professor: $435,000 - $465,000 Professor: $525,000 - $555,000 This pay range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. It does not include all components of the School of Medicine’s faculty compensation program or pay from participation in departmental incentive compensation programs. For more information about compensation and our wide-range of benefits, including housing assistance, please contact the hiring department. Stanford University has provided a pay range representing its good faith estimate of what the university reasonably expects to pay for the position upon hire. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Back to all jobs Apply Now First Name (required) 6e534759 Last Name (required) e35c0022 Email (required) 5b6d48c2 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign up for job alerts Job Alert ba6c0965 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. School School of Medicine School of Medicine Line University Medical Line University Tenure Line University Medical Line University Tenure Line Location Stanford University, California, United States Stanford University, California, United States First Name (required) 2310f252 Last Name (required) f44d0b16 Email (required) d3c0caec Subscribe Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Get Help Questions about a job or your application? Find out how to request help Equal Opportunity Stanford is an equal employment opportunity and affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Stanford welcomes applications from all who would bring additional dimensions to the University’s research, teaching and clinical missions. Consistent with its obligations under the law, the University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants and employees with disabilities. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process should contact disability.access@stanford.edu . Stanford University Stanford Home Maps & Directions Search Stanford Emergency Info Terms of Use Cookie Preferences Privacy Copyright Trademarks Non-Discrimination Accessibility © 2026 Stanford University. Stanford, California 94305. Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
